 ### [Ray-Layer Buffer Generation via Depth Peeling](9474-ray-layer-buffer-generation-via-depth-peeling.md)
-This method generates the ray-layer buffer for multi-layer volume ray casting by depth-peeling Volume of Interest (VOI) definitions and grouping the resulting fragments into ordered layers based on VOI priority and material ID. It differs from standard GPU-based Constructive Solid Geometry (CSG) by performing stencil-like tests inside a GPU program rather than using hardware stencil buffers, thereby preserving critical priority and ID information required for correct layer placement and optimization.
+Generates the ray-layer buffer by depth-peeling VOI definitions and grouping fragments into ordered layers based on priority and material ID. Chosen when complex VOI interactions (clipping, CSG, embedded objects) need to be resolved efficiently in a single rendering pass.
 
 ## Operating characteristics
--   **Time Complexity:** The multi-pass depth peeling cost is nearly ignorable compared to the ray casting cost [S1]. The algorithm shifts computational workload to layer sorting to keep the ray-casting loop as simple as possible [S1].
--   **Memory Complexity:** The ray-layer buffer stores only the Z value of the starting position and material ID per segment, reducing memory usage compared to methods storing full 3D texture coordinates [S1].
--   **Performance:** Using a single shared ray-layer buffer for the whole volume (with bricking) is more efficient and easier to debug than using a different ray-layer buffer for each brick [S1]. Compared to multi-pass depth peeling on intersecting volumes that raycasts each layer in a separate pass, this method stores peeling information in a single ray-layer buffer, requiring only one raycasting pass, which is more efficient for GPU hardware [S1].
--   **Strengths:** Supports interactive changing of shape and appearance of VOIs; handles complex CSG operations via priority numbers; integrates empty-space skipping, volume clipping, and embedding of opaque/semi-transparent objects [S1].
--   **Limitations:** Requires pre-processing to generate the ray-layer buffer; relies on GPU capabilities for depth peeling and texture fetching [S1].
--   **When to Use:** Medical imaging and scientific visualization software for interactive volume editing, cutaway views, and prioritized overlay of geometric masks, MPRs, and embedded objects [S1].
+*   **Time Complexity:** The multi-pass depth peeling cost is nearly ignorable compared to the ray casting cost [S1]. The algorithm shifts computational workload to layer sorting to keep the ray-casting loop simple [S1].
+*   **Memory Complexity:** Uses less memory than polygon-assisted ray casting because it stores only the Z-coordinate of the starting position, not full 3D texture coordinates [S1].
+*   **Performance:** More efficient for GPU hardware than multi-pass depth peeling methods that raycast each layer in a separate pass, as it requires only one raycasting pass [S1].
+*   **Strengths:** Unified framework for various enhancements (empty-space skipping, clipping, CSG, embedding); interactive editing support; simple ray-casting loop; bricking compatible [S1].
+*   **Limitations:** Requires preprocessing (depth peeling) to generate the ray-layer buffer; relies on GPU programmability for the peeling and ray casting stages [S1].
+*   **When to Use:** Medical imaging and scientific visualization software requiring interactive volume editing, cutaway views, and prioritized overlay of geometric masks, MPRs, and embedded objects [S1].
+*   **When Not to Use:** Simple volume rendering without complex VOI interactions or embedded objects, where standard ray marching may be simpler [S1].
 
 ## Background
-Multi-layer volume ray casting was presented by Wei Li from Siemens Corporate Research [S1]. It integrates various volume rendering enhancements into a unified framework, including empty-space skipping, volume clipping, embedding opaque and semi-transparent objects, and volume editing through CSG operations [S1]. The central idea is to consider all these objects as VOIs, each assigned a priority number to determine how overlapped regions are handled [S1]. All VOI information is encoded into a ray-layer buffer through depth peeling combined with layer simplification [S1].
+Multi-layer volume ray casting was introduced by Wei Li from Siemens Corporate Research [S1]. The method addresses the limitations of single-layer ray casting and polygon-assisted ray casting in handling complex spatial overlaps and embedded objects [S1]. It builds on the principles of depth peeling and GPU-based volume rendering, aiming to provide a general framework for interactive volume visualization with various enhancements [S1].
 
